00:09:50.880 --> 00:09:52.111
is like torture.

00:09:52.111 --> 00:09:52.944
I'm not a good reader.

00:09:52.944 --> 00:09:56.542
I've gotten better probably
'cause of the audiobook.

00:09:56.542 --> 00:10:00.245
It's unbelievably difficult
to get through for me,

00:10:00.245 --> 00:10:01.103
which is rare, 'cause I don't do

00:10:01.103 --> 00:10:02.912
a lot of things I don't like doing.

00:10:02.912 --> 00:10:04.405
But it feels so good when it's out

00:10:04.405 --> 00:10:06.713
and I know that it brings a lot of value.

00:10:06.713 --> 00:10:08.436
- It brings a tremendous amount of value

00:10:08.436 --> 00:10:10.391
and I think that for someone like you

00:10:10.391 --> 00:10:12.644
that's gotta be the most fulfilling part,

00:10:12.644 --> 00:10:15.183
is that you're really
bringing value to people.

00:10:15.183 --> 00:10:17.221
I see what people write on social media

00:10:17.221 --> 00:10:18.258
in the comments and stuff.

00:10:18.258 --> 00:10:19.450
That's gotta feel good.

00:10:19.450 --> 00:10:20.994
- Positive reinforcement's incredible.

00:10:20.994 --> 00:10:24.079
I hate when people are like,
oh, I love doing for others,

00:10:24.079 --> 00:10:26.154
and they try to position
it as this altruistic

00:10:26.154 --> 00:10:27.900
they're Gandhi shit.

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.716
That's fine, and I like it too.

00:10:29.716 --> 00:10:30.549
It's a nice feeling.

00:10:30.549 --> 00:10:31.726
It's an incredible legacy.

00:10:31.726 --> 00:10:32.559
- Yeah.

00:10:32.559 --> 00:10:36.500
- But no question I get an
adrenaline rush or euphoria,

00:10:36.500 --> 00:10:38.570
whatever the hell it is,
you know, I failed science.

00:10:38.570 --> 00:10:41.479
Whatever I'm getting from the
feedback, it's incredible.

00:10:41.479 --> 00:10:45.229
It's an incredibly
humbling, prideful feeling

00:10:47.665 --> 00:10:49.416
to have people say nice things

00:10:49.416 --> 00:10:50.965
and think that you brought them value.

00:10:50.965 --> 00:10:51.798
- Yeah, yeah.

00:10:51.798 --> 00:10:54.021
Tony Robbins talks about that contribution

00:10:54.021 --> 00:10:55.902
and that growth, you
know, the two spiritual

00:10:55.902 --> 00:10:57.918
kind of needs that we
have, and when you help

00:10:57.918 --> 00:10:59.265
other people you get both of them.

00:10:59.265 --> 00:11:00.518
- It's karma, man.
- Yeah.

00:11:00.518 --> 00:11:02.738
- Karma's practical.

00:11:02.738 --> 00:11:06.413
There is no formula that
isn't more obvious to me

00:11:06.413 --> 00:11:08.729
than doing the right
thing is the right thing.

00:11:08.729 --> 00:11:10.843
It will work itself out.

00:11:10.843 --> 00:11:13.824
Now, the reason people struggle
with doing the right thing

00:11:13.824 --> 00:11:16.929
is 'cause they do the
right thing with the intent

00:11:16.929 --> 00:11:18.417
of getting something back.

00:11:18.417 --> 00:11:21.118
They give with purpose of something else.

00:11:21.118 --> 00:11:22.248
- Yeah.
- So they're actually

00:11:22.248 --> 00:11:23.081
not giving.

00:11:23.081 --> 00:11:24.359
When you're actually just giving

00:11:24.359 --> 00:11:25.987
you're giving without expectation.

00:11:25.987 --> 00:11:26.820
- Right.

00:11:26.820 --> 00:11:28.822
- When people are like, no, it's not good,

00:11:28.822 --> 00:11:31.662
it's because they gave with the intent

00:11:31.662 --> 00:11:33.432
of then that person reciprocating

00:11:33.432 --> 00:11:35.298
something greater than they gave.

00:11:35.298 --> 00:11:37.822
Gary V, I'll drive you to the,

00:11:37.822 --> 00:11:40.025
I'll pick you up, I'll be a great, Gary,

00:11:40.025 --> 00:11:41.938
I'll be a great guy, I'll
pick you up at the airport

00:11:41.938 --> 00:11:43.779
and drive you to the hotel.

00:11:43.779 --> 00:11:46.963
I'm like, so I have to be
on for those 27 minutes

00:11:46.963 --> 00:11:50.086
and answer all your burning
desirable questions one on one?

00:11:50.086 --> 00:11:51.886
Now, I'm flattered.
- Yeah.

00:11:51.886 --> 00:11:55.833
- But don't position it, if
you said, can you do me a favor

00:11:55.833 --> 00:11:57.160
and let me pick you up--
- Yeah.

00:11:57.160 --> 00:11:59.879
- And answer 27, that's actually
how you get me to do it.

00:11:59.879 --> 00:12:00.712
- Yeah.

00:12:00.712 --> 00:12:03.169
- If somebody emailed me
instead of the bullshit of

00:12:03.169 --> 00:12:04.899
I'm gonna do you a favor,
I'm gonna buy you lunch,

00:12:04.899 --> 00:12:07.138
or drive you to the
airport, and they said, hey,

00:12:07.138 --> 00:12:08.579
can you do me a favor?

00:12:08.579 --> 00:12:11.298
I'll pick you up in the airport
and take you to the hotel

00:12:11.298 --> 00:12:13.924
and can you answer 14
questions, then I would say

00:12:13.924 --> 00:12:16.250
that's authentic, I'm in.
- Yeah.

00:12:16.250 --> 00:12:19.339
I think somebody pretty
smart said bring value,

00:12:19.339 --> 00:12:21.666
bring value, bring value, then ask.

00:12:21.666 --> 00:12:22.732
- And thank you for saying that,

00:12:22.732 --> 00:12:25.016
and ask is the key,
'cause I think most people

00:12:25.016 --> 00:12:27.333
even the ones that bought
into me and like me

00:12:27.333 --> 00:12:29.906
and kind of read Jab, Jab,
Jab, Right Hook and got there

00:12:29.906 --> 00:12:32.738
thought it meant bring value,
bring value, bring value,

00:12:32.738 --> 00:12:34.639
and then take something back.

00:12:34.639 --> 00:12:37.204
You have to understand
that the second you start

00:12:37.204 --> 00:12:40.131
creating expectations
you become vulnerable.

00:12:40.131 --> 00:12:40.964
- Yeah.

00:12:42.025 --> 00:12:43.433
That makes sense.

00:12:43.433 --> 00:12:45.312
So tell us about K-Swiss.

00:12:45.312 --> 00:12:49.074
- K-Swiss, so, I got a random
email almost two years ago

00:12:49.074 --> 00:12:50.657
that said I'm the president of K-Swiss

00:12:50.657 --> 00:12:53.031
and we'd like to made a Gary V sneaker.

00:12:53.031 --> 00:12:54.290
I liked that email.

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:55.123
(laughing)

00:12:55.123 --> 00:12:57.364
And I was curious if he was real.

00:12:57.364 --> 00:12:58.197
- Yeah.
- Meaning,

00:12:58.197 --> 00:12:59.075
I didn't think he was a joke.

00:12:59.075 --> 00:13:00.087
I knew he was the actual president.

00:13:00.087 --> 00:13:03.156
I was worried that it was gonna
be like an influencer thing.

00:13:03.156 --> 00:13:04.556
There was a sneaker that they already made

00:13:04.556 --> 00:13:06.490
and I could be like Gary V says.

00:13:06.490 --> 00:13:08.122
I was like fuck that.
- Just slap your face on it.

00:13:08.122 --> 00:13:08.955
- Right.
- Yeah.

00:13:08.955 --> 00:13:10.684
- No, slap my face is
what I was looking for.

00:13:10.684 --> 00:13:13.189
I wanted to put my signature
and have a Gary V sneaker

00:13:13.189 --> 00:13:14.022
and this isn't one.

00:13:14.022 --> 00:13:14.855
This is just a different thing.

00:13:14.855 --> 00:13:17.896
But I, there was two
things that mattered to me.

00:13:17.896 --> 00:13:19.532
One, I thought it was cool.

00:13:19.532 --> 00:13:21.317
I thought if I could
make a successful sneaker

00:13:21.317 --> 00:13:23.585
then I'd be the first
entrepreneur for the mainstream.

00:13:23.585 --> 00:13:26.162
I wanna buy nostalgic brands in my career.

00:13:26.162 --> 00:13:28.141
K-Swiss was a nostalgic brand,

00:13:28.141 --> 00:13:30.864
and I also liked that
it could have failed.

00:13:30.864 --> 00:13:34.598
I liked the fear of
falling on my face with it.

00:13:34.598 --> 00:13:35.431
- Yeah.

00:13:35.431 --> 00:13:37.373
- 'Cause I thought that was a good energy

00:13:37.373 --> 00:13:40.567
and I liked the idea of
giving my friends ammo

00:13:40.567 --> 00:13:41.694
to make fun of me.

00:13:41.694 --> 00:13:44.513
Literally one of the
reasons I signed the deal

00:13:44.513 --> 00:13:46.973
is I romanticized this
vision 28 years from now--

00:13:46.973 --> 00:13:48.025
- Yeah.
- Where my friends are like,

00:13:48.025 --> 00:13:50.397
hey, remember when you were a dick

00:13:50.397 --> 00:13:51.905
and you thought you could sell sneakers?

00:13:51.905 --> 00:13:52.738
You idiot!

00:13:52.738 --> 00:13:53.571
(laughing)

00:13:53.571 --> 00:13:56.275
I think that's a good idea
to always push yourself

00:13:56.275 --> 00:13:59.974
where the down side is you
can make fun yourself--

00:13:59.974 --> 00:14:00.994
- Yeah.
- And move on.

00:14:00.994 --> 00:14:02.177
That's how that deal felt.

00:14:02.177 --> 00:14:03.459
It went really, really well.

00:14:03.459 --> 00:14:06.806
We have the one coming
out, the 003 in July.

00:14:06.806 --> 00:14:08.279
It's gonna be a challenge.

00:14:08.279 --> 00:14:10.196
We made a lot of pairs.

00:14:11.244 --> 00:14:12.585
Me and the team were kind of looking it.

00:14:12.585 --> 00:14:15.323
It's gonna take work, but I
think it's gonna be successful.

00:14:15.323 --> 00:14:18.474
- Yeah, did you get any
advice from Daymond John?

00:14:18.474 --> 00:14:19.446
- I didn't.
- Okay.

00:14:19.446 --> 00:14:20.540
- Though Daymond's awesome.
- Yeah.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:23.502
- First of all, I tend not to take advice

00:14:23.502 --> 00:14:25.727
because I think it's ego.
- Yeah.

00:14:25.727 --> 00:14:28.360
- But I've got to figure, or,
but I also think it's slow.

00:14:28.360 --> 00:14:31.638
I don't like it, I like taking
advice from the audience.

00:14:31.638 --> 00:14:35.014
I'd much rather read every
single person's comment

00:14:35.014 --> 00:14:39.200
to this interview than take
advice from somebody fancy.

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:40.816
- Yeah.
- I really mean that.

00:14:40.816 --> 00:14:41.649
I really mean that.

00:14:41.649 --> 00:14:44.240
And then, by the way, that's how I learn.

00:14:44.240 --> 00:14:45.872
I spew advice all day.

00:14:45.872 --> 00:14:48.215
If you get value from it, amazing.

00:14:48.215 --> 00:14:50.126
If you don't, I love when people are like,

00:14:50.126 --> 00:14:52.287
hey bro, sorry, but I
don't watch your stuff.

00:14:52.287 --> 00:14:54.292
I'm like, great.

00:14:54.292 --> 00:14:55.416
I wouldn't watch it either.

00:14:55.416 --> 00:14:57.216
(laughing)

00:14:57.216 --> 00:14:58.160
- What do you think is one of

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:00.273
the biggest misconceptions about you?

00:15:00.273 --> 00:15:02.190
Speaking of the public.

00:15:03.176 --> 00:15:06.730
- I think at first, for all
the people in the audience

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:10.442
just now, who never heard
of me, who are here to see

00:15:10.442 --> 00:15:14.310
Tony Robbins and I'm on
the thing, it's very easy

00:15:14.310 --> 00:15:16.508
in public settings, interviews--

00:15:16.508 --> 00:15:17.341
- Yeah.

00:15:17.341 --> 00:15:21.342
- And on stage for my competitive
bravado to be my lead.

00:15:21.342 --> 00:15:22.175
- Yeah.

00:15:22.175 --> 00:15:24.858
- Steve, for example, let's
use him in this interview,

00:15:24.858 --> 00:15:27.923
he knows me, he knows the
part, 'cause he watched

00:15:27.923 --> 00:15:30.009
Vayner being built and he
knows how I've handled him,

00:15:30.009 --> 00:15:33.675
he knows the far more
compassionate, kind (mumbles),

00:15:33.675 --> 00:15:36.592
the warm and fuzzy mama type of me.

00:15:37.722 --> 00:15:40.286
I'm a very interesting contradiction.

00:15:40.286 --> 00:15:43.619
I am super kind of emotional, soft, not,

00:15:45.913 --> 00:15:48.518
because I'm not financially driven--

00:15:48.518 --> 00:15:49.351
- Yeah.

00:15:49.351 --> 00:15:52.426
- I don't have that cut throat
I'm making a money decision.

00:15:52.426 --> 00:15:53.920
Sorry, man, it's just business.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:55.634
I'll drag out firing someone.

00:15:55.634 --> 00:15:57.082
I'll try to put them on.

00:15:57.082 --> 00:15:59.197
I'm always there for them.

00:15:59.197 --> 00:16:02.811
So, I think the biggest
misconception is that

00:16:02.811 --> 00:16:05.366
between my cursing, my Jersey energy,

00:16:05.366 --> 00:16:08.297
and my bravado, that I'm cut throat,

00:16:08.297 --> 00:16:11.032
I'm winner take all, I'm fuck you,

00:16:11.032 --> 00:16:14.272
and I think that there's a
part of me that's like that,

00:16:14.272 --> 00:16:17.319
the one that's, when, you
know who I think I am?

00:16:17.319 --> 00:16:20.342
I think I'm that athlete
that on the field will

00:16:20.342 --> 00:16:24.500
trip you, will hold on every
play, will fuckin' cheat.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:26.295
- Poke your eyes out.

00:16:26.295 --> 00:16:27.884
- Yeah, poke your eye.

00:16:27.884 --> 00:16:30.801
But, when the game's over--
- Yeah.

00:16:30.801 --> 00:16:32.678
- I'm the first one to
come and high five you,

00:16:32.678 --> 00:16:34.482
ask you about how's Karen,

00:16:34.482 --> 00:16:37.516
put in a recommendation
for your grand kid to get,

00:16:37.516 --> 00:16:40.031
your kid to get into
the school I went into,

00:16:40.031 --> 00:16:42.880
if I'm a good player from Ohio State.

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.075
I think when I'm on the field
I want to destroy your face

00:16:46.075 --> 00:16:48.706
but in real life I want you to have

00:16:48.706 --> 00:16:50.261
the happiest life of all time.

00:16:50.261 --> 00:16:52.076
- Yeah, empathy.
- Empathy.

00:16:52.076 --> 00:16:55.214
- Yeah, just a couple
more questions for you.

00:16:55.214 --> 00:16:58.307
What do you think about
the investment side

00:16:58.307 --> 00:17:00.271
of the cannabis business?

00:17:00.271 --> 00:17:01.554
- I'm very bullish on it.

00:17:01.554 --> 00:17:02.390
- Are you?
- Yes.

00:17:02.390 --> 00:17:04.242
- You think it's gonna continue to be,

00:17:04.242 --> 00:17:05.879
I mean, they're saying
like 20 billion dollars

00:17:05.879 --> 00:17:07.942
in the next couple of years, yeah.

00:17:07.942 --> 00:17:09.337
- Here's the problem.

00:17:09.337 --> 00:17:12.058
There'll be a couple Philip Morrises.

00:17:12.058 --> 00:17:16.267
And then there's gonna be
a trillion shitty Morrises.

00:17:16.267 --> 00:17:19.444
The amount of money that's
about to be lost in mainstream

00:17:19.444 --> 00:17:23.183
cannabis is gonna be as
much as is gonna be made.

00:17:23.183 --> 00:17:26.909
People don't understand gold rushes.

00:17:26.909 --> 00:17:28.760
Not everybody finds gold.

00:17:28.760 --> 00:17:29.593
- Yeah.

00:17:29.593 --> 00:17:30.873
- There's a lot of hucksters in it.

00:17:30.873 --> 00:17:33.179
By the nature of the
product, you've got a bunch

00:17:33.179 --> 00:17:36.026
of fuckin' people in it
that don't wanna work.

00:17:36.026 --> 00:17:36.859
- Yeah.
- Really.

00:17:36.859 --> 00:17:39.031
You got a bunch of people
that think it's a gold rush.

00:17:39.031 --> 00:17:40.809
It's easy, it's free money.

00:17:40.809 --> 00:17:42.137
There's no such thing.
- Yeah.

00:17:42.137 --> 00:17:44.225
- So, I'm very bullish
on it, just like I was

00:17:44.225 --> 00:17:45.960
very bullish on the internet.

00:17:45.960 --> 00:17:47.615
A lot of people have made a lot of money

00:17:47.615 --> 00:17:49.159
and way more lost a lot of money.

00:17:49.159 --> 00:17:50.715
That's what's gonna happen to cannabis.

00:17:50.715 --> 00:17:52.661
- Yeah, what about e-sports?

00:17:52.661 --> 00:17:53.748
- Obsessed.
- Yeah.

00:17:53.748 --> 00:17:58.415
- I think e-sports easily is
a top four sport in America

00:18:00.723 --> 00:18:02.018
in seven years.
- Wow.

00:18:02.018 --> 00:18:04.229
- Soon, rapid, and at scale.

00:18:04.229 --> 00:18:06.462
I think American football,
even with concussions,

00:18:06.462 --> 00:18:10.264
American football,
basketball, soccer, e-sports.

00:18:10.264 --> 00:18:12.691
E-sports is way bigger than hockey

00:18:12.691 --> 00:18:14.858
and golf and other things.

00:18:16.266 --> 00:18:17.997
- It's crazy, I mean, we
were just talking about it.

00:18:17.997 --> 00:18:20.660
They filled this arena,
like 20,000 people,

00:18:20.660 --> 00:18:23.967
for Dream Hack here just
to watch them play games.

00:18:23.967 --> 00:18:26.913
- Oh yeah, in Korea 100,000 people.

00:18:26.913 --> 00:18:30.409
Look at Ninja, 600,000
people concurrent streams.

00:18:30.409 --> 00:18:31.929
This is real life shit.

00:18:31.929 --> 00:18:32.762
- Yeah.

00:18:32.762 --> 00:18:34.499
Yeah, it's happening right now.

00:18:34.499 --> 00:18:37.362
Just real quickly, talk
about some of the advantages

00:18:37.362 --> 00:18:40.385
of podcasting as a way to communicate,

00:18:40.385 --> 00:18:42.989
and then also you're
really big on voice, right?

00:18:42.989 --> 00:18:43.822
- I am.

00:18:43.822 --> 00:18:46.567
Podcasting is audio and audio is passive

00:18:46.567 --> 00:18:49.406
and audio is convenient
and we love convenience

00:18:49.406 --> 00:18:51.896
and there's a lot of people
who now listen to my podcast

00:18:51.896 --> 00:18:54.144
instead of watching my
videos 'cause it's easier

00:18:54.144 --> 00:18:56.957
while you're running
or while you're driving

00:18:56.957 --> 00:18:58.359
or while you're on a plane,

00:18:58.359 --> 00:19:00.866
because you have to give
the attention to the screen.

00:19:00.866 --> 00:19:03.750
I'm a very, very big fan of audio

00:19:03.750 --> 00:19:07.617
and I think we saw pictures
and videos have their day

00:19:07.617 --> 00:19:10.794
and will always have their day
but have done incredibly well

00:19:10.794 --> 00:19:13.217
in the last seven years on the internet.

00:19:13.217 --> 00:19:15.646
I think you're gonna see
audio have an incredible,

00:19:15.646 --> 00:19:18.673
we're seeing it, and more,
and to your point about voice,

00:19:18.673 --> 00:19:21.211
AI, machine learning, intelligence,

00:19:21.211 --> 00:19:24.840
speaking to Alexa, speaking
to Google Assistant,

00:19:24.840 --> 00:19:27.660
these are gonna be
incredible technologies.

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:29.447
Voice is the next platform.
- So easy.

00:19:29.447 --> 00:19:30.556
- No friction.

00:19:30.556 --> 00:19:32.749
- Yeah, I mean, how
could it get any easier

00:19:32.749 --> 00:19:34.290
unless you just think about it.

00:19:34.290 --> 00:19:35.868
- Think about this.

00:19:35.868 --> 00:19:37.512
For Steve to be here he's probably

00:19:37.512 --> 00:19:39.239
talked to somebody on my team.

00:19:39.239 --> 00:19:42.098
The fact that Steve could
have said in six years,

00:19:42.098 --> 00:19:45.084
Alexa, I wanna see Gary
when he's in Denver.

00:19:45.084 --> 00:19:46.838
Alexa's like, cool,
let me get back to you.

00:19:46.838 --> 00:19:49.446
On my end Alexa's like,
hey, Steve Campbell wants

00:19:49.446 --> 00:19:50.609
to see you, you cool with that?

00:19:50.609 --> 00:19:51.442
I'm like, yes.

00:19:51.442 --> 00:19:55.031
Alexa then or Google then taps
into both of our calendars,

00:19:55.031 --> 00:19:58.203
sees when the timing's
right, and books it for us,

00:19:58.203 --> 00:20:00.310
and by the way, it might
have been that we shouldn't

00:20:00.310 --> 00:20:01.605
